WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.240
If you want to become exceptional in

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:06.480
anything you do, you need to stop

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.559
following the same surface level advice

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.400
everyone else follows. How will that

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:13.280
make you different? You have to start

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.480
understanding how the top 1% actually

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.560
operate. All through my life as a CEO,

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.800
board member, and investor for some of

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.039
the most innovative [music] companies in

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.519
America, I've seen what works at the

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:27.680
highest level of business. And there are

00:00:25.519 --> 00:00:30.800
four principles that separate the

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.520
absolute best from everyone else. These

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.680
principles aren't about working harder.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.800
They're about digging deeper into your

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.960
biology, your natural abilities, and the

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.480
systems you can build to make your

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.320
success inevitable. And this is what you

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.125
need to know. The first principle of

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.440
peak performance is understanding your

00:00:49.125 --> 00:00:54.079
[music] anti-talent. The misconception

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.000
that derails careers is the following.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:58.239
this belief that you can fix anything

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.120
and everything if you just grind hard

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:04.000
enough. Sure, I can improve many skills

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:07.040
with practice. But there is a part of me

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.720
that can never scale no matter how hard

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:13.280
I try. And I call that my anti-talent.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.240
All of us have it. Our anti-talent is

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.960
not our weakness. That you can fix with

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:22.000
hard work. Anti-talent is something that

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.840
does not respond to effort at all. Let's

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.640
look at some examples to make this

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.280
clear. [music]

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.640
First, let's look at Keanu Reeves. I

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:33.011
mean, today he is an iconic actor. But

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.799
early in his career, critics destroyed

00:01:33.011 --> 00:01:39.119
[music] him for being wooden and stiff.

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:41.040
He was trying to do dramatic roles, but

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:44.799
he was no Robert Dairo. [music]

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.880
Emotional range, dialogue delivery, that

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:49.200
was actually his anti-talent. [music]

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.200
But he didn't try to fix it. Eventually,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.240
he realized that he could engineer

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:58.320
around it. In John Wick 4, he speaks

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.759
only 380 [music] words in three hours.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.079
>> Maybe not.

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:04.719
>> He moved away from his anti-talent to

00:02:02.079 --> 00:02:07.726
where he dominated [music] physicality,

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:10.080
precision, presence, a kind of zen

00:02:07.726 --> 00:02:12.560
[music] calmness. And the result, the

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.879
Matrix trilogy and the John Wick

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.840
franchises have grossed nearly $3

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:20.080
billion. And you know the same is true

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.040
at the highest level of business. Think

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:25.760
about Satyana Deella. He took over

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.360
Microsoft after Steve Balmer [music] and

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:32.000
the world expected the same bombastic

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:34.879
tablepounding alpha male CEO with an

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:38.560
MBA. But that would have been Satya's

00:02:34.879 --> 00:02:42.640
anti-talent. He was a technologist who

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:45.599
quoted poetry. He was thoughtful. He was

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:48.480
strategic. Steve Balmer loved a culture

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:50.640
of competition. Satya moved Microsoft

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.800
toward a culture of empathy and

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.519
innovation. What looked like his

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.760
softness became Microsoft's hardest

00:02:55.519 --> 00:03:00.879
competitive advantage, leading to more

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.200
than $3 trillion of market cap and

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:06.480
growing. So that's how you navigate

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.400
around your anti-talent. And how do you

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.200
identify your anti-talent? You should

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:15.120
ask yourself three questions. Number

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:17.760
one, what drains you that energizes

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.400
others? Number two, what have you worked

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.480
at for years with very little

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.040
improvement to show for it? And number

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.200
three, what do people compliment you on?

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.920
Things that you never worked on. Now,

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:33.200
the first two are anti-talents. [music]

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:36.560
The third one is your innate talent. Let

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:39.120
me share with you my anti-talent. After

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.400
business school, I had this dream, a

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.480
single purpose in life. I wanted to be a

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:47.519
management consultant at one of the top

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:50.720
three, McKenzie, BCG or Bane. It sounds

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:53.200
so silly now, but I practiced and I

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.440
practiced case interviews, case studies,

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.080
business models, frameworks, all of it

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.200
for hours and hours. And I interviewed

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:04.080
10 times across all three firms. Failed

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:07.200
every single time. never got hired

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:10.959
because what came easily to others felt

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.040
hund times harder for me. My brain just

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:15.760
didn't work [music] the way management

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.479
consultants do and I used to feel so

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:21.199
insecure about it. But clearly my

00:04:18.479 --> 00:04:24.080
talents lie somewhere else. So please

00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:26.960
remember a path does not open up simply

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.040
because you want it to. Some paths

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.960
aren't yours to walk on. You know my

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.199
spiritual master used to say this a

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.000
river always finds a way around a

00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:39.040
mountain and gets to the ocean always

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.440
and your talent will find its way too.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.919
The second principle is understanding

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.280
your biological balance sheet. Now you

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:47.199
could never run your company without

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.199
knowing your assets or liabilities,

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:52.639
right? So why would you run your life

00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:56.479
that way? In 2011, researchers analyzed

00:04:52.639 --> 00:04:59.360
over 1,000 parole cases. These were

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:02.400
serious judges making serious decisions

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:05.520
on serious cases. But the data revealed

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.960
a terrifying pattern. If your case was

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:13.120
heard at 8:50 a.m. in the morning, your

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:15.759
chances of parole were 65%. But at 12:50

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:19.120
p.m. instead, right before lunch, your

00:05:15.759 --> 00:05:22.080
chance dropped to near 0%. And after the

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.880
meal break, approval rates jump back

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:27.520
right back to 65%. Now, these judges

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.320
weren't corrupt. They weren't biased.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:33.440
They were just chemically depleted. When

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:35.440
their decision fuel, glucose, ran out,

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.680
their brain defaulted to the path of

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:41.039
least resistance, and they said no to

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.360
parole. So, your biology isn't separate

00:05:41.039 --> 00:05:45.759
from your performance. It is your

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:48.240
performance. But the good thing is your

00:05:45.759 --> 00:05:50.960
biology runs on the same rules as a

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.840
balance sheet. It has assets and it has

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.840
liability. But unfortunately, most of us

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:58.240
confuse which one is which. For

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.479
instance, many hardworking individuals I

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.880
know feel that they need to cut down on

00:06:00.479 --> 00:06:05.520
sleep because their workload is so heavy

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:07.600
and huge. But sleep is the most

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.400
essential part to grow your cognitive

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:13.759
capacity. So sleep is an asset, [music]

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.919
but we treat it as a liability. Now, of

00:06:13.759 --> 00:06:18.319
course, if you are at an entry-level job

00:06:15.919 --> 00:06:20.880
in investment banking or a hedge fund or

00:06:18.319 --> 00:06:22.800
a tech startup, it's a price of entry.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.720
You'll have to work hard. But even

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:29.440
there, you reward it for clarity, not

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.240
emotional chaos. You forget that your

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:34.639
bosses and your investors [music] aren't

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.120
paying you for stamina. AI has more

00:06:34.639 --> 00:06:39.360
stamina than anyone. They're paying you

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:41.840
for your judgment. [music] So, what are

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.829
the actionable takeaways? Let me share

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.039
two ideas.

00:06:42.829 --> 00:06:45.440
>> [music]

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:49.199
>> Number one, if you want to protect your

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:52.880
focus, then reduce your decision cost

00:06:49.199 --> 00:06:55.600
asset liability. When I was a CEO, I was

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:58.960
always chasing the clock, always late.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:01.919
So, I created a standard meal for myself

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:04.479
for lunch. Some protein, some greens,

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:07.199
slow carbs, boring stuff, but good for

00:07:04.479 --> 00:07:09.520
you. And I ate the same meal every day

00:07:07.199 --> 00:07:11.919
at the same time, unless when I was

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:14.240
traveling or when I was with customers.

00:07:11.919 --> 00:07:17.120
Why? because I wanted to remove the

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:20.240
cognitive clutter of deciding what to

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:23.360
eat, where to go, where to order, when

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:25.599
to eat, none of that. I wanted to focus

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:29.039
on what mattered. The second idea is

00:07:25.599 --> 00:07:31.599
even simpler. Protect your sleep. CDC

00:07:29.039 --> 00:07:34.575
reports show that one night of short

00:07:31.599 --> 00:07:35.759
sleep drops working memory by 20 to 30%.

00:07:34.575 --> 00:07:39.280
[music]

00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:42.000
So, regular and regulated sleep is

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.720
non-negotiable for high performers. So

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:48.319
find your circadian rhythm and lock it

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:51.440
in. A bankrupt body cannot support a

00:07:48.319 --> 00:07:53.520
wealthy mind. Next up is the simulation

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:56.560
advantage. For the first time in

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:59.840
history, you can fail privately over and

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:03.280
over again before you fail publicly.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:06.160
Think about F1 drivers. There are only

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.560
about 20 of them in the world who get to

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:12.080
perform at that peak level. And their

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:14.800
careers are decided by tiny number of

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:18.160
races every year. And even inside those

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:20.639
races, a tiny number of decisions like

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:23.280
when to break, when to overtake, when to

00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:26.960
pit. Those make a difference between

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:29.599
victory and defeat and sometimes even

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:32.000
between life and death. So what do they

00:08:29.599 --> 00:08:35.039
do? They simulate those moments every

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:37.279
day, year round. Your careers and

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:39.519
ventures work the same way too. There

00:08:37.279 --> 00:08:42.719
are handful of moments, you know, one

00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:44.720
negotiation, one board presentation, one

00:08:42.719 --> 00:08:46.959
judgment that could define your entire

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:50.480
trajectory. I remember at least two

00:08:46.959 --> 00:08:52.800
times when a single off-the-cuff answer

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:55.440
and decision damaged my career and

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.440
credibility in a second. It's etched in

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.959
my brain and every time I think about

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:03.279
it, I still just shake my head in sheer

00:09:00.959 --> 00:09:05.279
disbelief. And that happens to all of

00:09:03.279 --> 00:09:07.839
us. That's why you shouldn't practice

00:09:05.279 --> 00:09:10.560
those moments while they're happening.

00:09:07.839 --> 00:09:13.200
Peak performers never stop rehearsing

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:15.600
those moments. Neuroscience shows us the

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.600
way. For decades, it's been known that

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.240
simulation activates nearly the same

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:22.800
neural circuits as the real thing. Think

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:25.279
about fighter pilots [music] or Olympic

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:26.560
athletes or even big budget movie

00:09:25.279 --> 00:09:29.440
directors nowadays. [music]

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:32.560
They all use simulators to plan and

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:34.720
visualize those critical moments so

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:38.240
intensely. [music] so meticulously and

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:41.600
so vividly that their brains light up as

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:44.640
if they're already inside that arena.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.880
And in today's AI era, a golfer can

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:49.519
practice her swing and upload the video

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:51.680
to an AI model that would analyze her

00:09:49.519 --> 00:09:54.000
swing and [music] tell her exactly how

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:56.160
to improve. You can even simulate

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.720
interviews or an important conversation

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:01.360
that you're about to have. Ask your AI

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:03.839
co-pilot to argue back, push you,

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:06.160
challenge your assumptions, even [music]

00:10:03.839 --> 00:10:08.720
escalate your emotional temperature. It

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:12.160
will do it. It can play the role of an

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:14.399
admirer or an adversary. That's the

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:16.880
simulation advantage. According to Jim

00:10:14.399 --> 00:10:17.760
Sabinius, who teaches negotiations at

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:21.200
Harvard, [music]

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:23.519
the setup is 80% of the game. It's all

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.079
about the prep. So, here's how you can

00:10:23.519 --> 00:10:27.920
use this tonight. Number one, pick one

00:10:26.079 --> 00:10:29.839
highstakes moment that's coming up.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:33.839
Let's say it's an interview or [music] a

00:10:29.839 --> 00:10:36.320
pitch or a date. Number two, practice

00:10:33.839 --> 00:10:38.800
with [music] AI. And don't just type.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.640
Turn on the voice mode. Have an actual

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.200
spoken conversation. [music] When you

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:45.600
hear a voice challenging your answers in

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:48.079
real time, it triggers the same

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:50.240
biological stress response as the actual

00:10:48.079 --> 00:10:52.959
room. Practice it with three different

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:55.279
AI personas. The first version, a

00:10:52.959 --> 00:10:57.760
friendly AI that wants you to succeed.

00:10:55.279 --> 00:11:00.640
That helps you practice confidence. The

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:02.640
second persona, AI who thinks you're not

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:05.200
really ready for this. That will help

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.279
you make your case stronger. And the

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:09.839
third persona, pick a completely

00:11:07.279 --> 00:11:12.160
irrational boss who just does not want

00:11:09.839 --> 00:11:14.800
to hear what you have to say. That will

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:16.240
help you practice calm and composure

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:18.800
under pressure. [music] And after

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:21.519
practicing with each of these personas,

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:23.920
every version, ask yourself, what

00:11:21.519 --> 00:11:26.480
surprised me about my behavior? What

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:28.480
threw me off? What can I do better? This

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:30.880
way, when you walk into the actual

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.560
highstakes room, you're walking into a

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:35.600
rerun. [music] By the time you enter

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:37.680
that room, the meeting is already over

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:40.560
in your head. The fourth and final

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:43.680
principle is what I call virtuoso

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:46.320
silence. There is a fascinating 2024

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:48.800
study on decisionmaking. It found that

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.360
if you make decisions under pressure

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:52.560
while you have high internal neural

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:55.519
noise, [music]

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:57.839
you perform dramatically worse, even if

00:11:55.519 --> 00:12:00.000
your IQ and experience [music] are

00:11:57.839 --> 00:12:03.040
identical to the person next to you.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.748
Why? Because your brain has two

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:08.320
competing priorities. The signal,

00:12:05.748 --> 00:12:11.040
[music] which is the task relevant info,

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:13.608
and the noise. everything else. High

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:15.279
performers don't have better brains.

00:12:13.608 --> 00:12:18.000
[music] They've trained themselves to

00:12:15.279 --> 00:12:20.480
have less noise. And the noise comes

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:22.240
from trying to do a lot more than what

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:24.399
you actually need to do. [music] Peak

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:26.639
performers are exactly like those

00:12:24.399 --> 00:12:29.600
virtuoso musicians. Quieter [music]

00:12:26.639 --> 00:12:32.399
on the inside, gravitating to silence

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:34.800
over static. So, what can you do today?

00:12:32.399 --> 00:12:38.160
You can use this three-step protocol.

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:40.959
Stop, drop, swap. This is a

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:44.079
microtransition protocol to use the

00:12:40.959 --> 00:12:46.800
silence to reset your [music] state.

00:12:44.079 --> 00:12:49.519
Number one, the stop. Finish the

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:51.440
previous block completely. Fully close

00:12:49.519 --> 00:12:54.320
the [music] tab. Close the book, the

